# Engineering standards

Apply when writing and reviewing code in this repo. Full review checklist: [docs/references/google-eng-practices.md](docs/references/google-eng-practices.md) ([google/eng-practices](https://github.com/google/eng-practices)).

## Change authoring

- **Minimal scope:** Smallest correct change; no drive-by refactors or unrelated edits. Prefer **small CLs**—one main concern per PR when practical.
- **Simplicity:** Prefer the straightforward solution; avoid premature abstraction and extra layers. Do not build for speculative future requirements.
- **DRY:** Do not copy-paste logic; reuse existing helpers when they already fit. Extract shared code only when duplication is real and the abstraction is obvious—do not invent one-off utilities for a single use.
- **Conventions:** Match surrounding naming, structure, and patterns; extend existing code before adding parallel implementations.
- **PR descriptions:** Explain *what*, *why*, and how tested—not only which files changed ([CL descriptions](https://github.com/google/eng-practices/blob/master/review/developer/cl-descriptions.md)).

## Tests, comments, documentation

- **Tests:** Add tests when they protect real behavior; skip tests that only restate the implementation. Add tests in the same change as production code when possible.
- **Comments:** Explain *why*, not *what*; remove obsolete TODOs. If code needs a long *what* comment, simplify the code.
- **Documentation:** Update READMEs or module docs when behavior, setup, or usage changes.

## Code review mindset (agents reviewing PRs)

- **Code health:** Each merge should improve overall maintainability, not add small degradations.
- **Continuous improvement:** Do not block on perfection; flag important issues clearly and use **`Nit:`** for optional polish.
- **Context:** Consider the whole file and system—not only the diff hunk.
- **Good practices:** Note what was done well, not only problems.

Language-specific style: follow repo tooling and [Google style guides](https://google.github.io/styleguide/) when no project config exists yet.
